      "name": "ComputeDualChannelSha1",
      "signature": "uint ComputeDualChannelSha1(uint pOutputBuffer, char * pHexString)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "uint",
      "comment": "Computes SHA-1 hashes for even and odd character channels of a hex string.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize loop counter to zero and validate character count is non-zero\n2. Scan input hex string to skip null bytes by advancing pointer and decrementing count\n3. Check if character count is odd; if so, skip first character and decrement count\n4. Divide remaining count by 2 (right shift by 1) to get half-length for each channel\n5. Call AllocateMemory with half-length size and source file marker (0x6ff3b030)\n6. If allocation fails, return zero; otherwise proceed to even-channel processing\n7. Copy every 2nd byte starting at index 0 (even characters) to decoded buffer\n8. Initialize SHA-1 state variables: A=0x67452301, B=0xefcdab89, C=0x98badcfe, D=0x10325476, E=0xc3d2e1f0\n9. Initialize SHA-1 count variables: CountLo=0, CountHi=0\n10. Call Sha1_UpdateBuffer to process even-character decoded data\n11. Call Sha1_Finalize to generate final even-channel hash (20 bytes)\n12. Store 20-byte hash result to output buffer at even offsets (stride of 2)\n13. Copy every 2nd byte starting at index 1 (odd characters) to decoded buffer\n14. Reinitialize SHA-1 state variables with same constants for odd-channel\n15. Call Sha1_UpdateBuffer to process odd-character decoded data\n16. Call Sha1_Finalize to generate final odd-channel hash\n17. Store 20-byte hash result to output buffer at odd offsets (stride of 2)\n18. Delete allocated decoded buffer via DeleteObjectTrampoline\n19. Return output buffer address on success\n\nParameters:\n- pOutputBuffer (uint): Address of 40-byte output array (20 bytes per channel for dual SHA-1 results)\n- pHexString (char*): Pointer to hex character string input; characters are split into even and odd indices for dual-channel processing\n\nReturns:\n- uint: Output buffer address on success, 0 if memory allocation fails\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If input character count is 0, function returns immediately with count=0 (no processing)\n- Null bytes in hex string are skipped by advancing pointer until non-null character found\n- Odd count causes first character to be skipped entirely (count decremented, pointer incremented)\n- SHA-1 state constants are standard FIPS 180-1 initialization values: 0x67452301, 0xefcdab89, 0x98badcfe, 0x10325476, 0xc3d2e1f0\n- Even-channel hash occupies output buffer offsets 0, 2, 4, ..., 38 (20 bytes at stride 2)\n- Odd-channel hash occupies output buffer offsets 1, 3, 5, ..., 39 (20 bytes at stride 2)\n- Decoded buffer is temporary working memory allocated for each channel's character data\n\nStructure Layout:\nOffset | Size | Field Name | Type | Description\n0      | 4    | pOutputBuffer | uint | Output array base address (40 bytes total)\n4      | 4    | pHexString | char* | Pointer to null-terminated hex string",
