      "name": "ProcessCryptoObjectWithHash",
      "signature": "void ProcessCryptoObjectWithHash(void * pCryptoContext, ObjectData * pObjectData, uint dwHashValue)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined",
      "comment": "Processes cryptographic object data by hashing and validating content.\\n\\nAlgorithm:\\n1. Hash first portion of crypto context (bytes 0x00-0x1f) using MD5\\n2. Hash second portion of crypto context (bytes 0x20-0x3f) using MD5\\n3. Process SHA1 input from source buffer into context+0x40\\n4. Import object data from input buffer\\n5. Initialize cryptographic object with null value\\n6. Execute ordinal 628 (verify/process operation)\\n7. Destroy temporary object\\n8. Export processed data to output buffer (0x20 byte max)\\n9. If bytes written < 0x20, zero-fill remaining buffer\\n\\nParameters:\\npCryptoContext (void*) - Pointer to 0xbc byte cryptographic context structure\\n  Layout: +0x00-+0x1f (first hash), +0x20-+0x3f (second hash),\\n          +0x40-+0x5f (SHA1 buffer), +0x60-+0x7f (output buffer)\\npObjectData (ObjectData*) - Pointer to ObjectData structure with status and object pointers\\nhashValue (uint) - Hash value (typically from hash transformation) to use as seed\\n\\nReturns:\\nvoid - Function modifies pCryptoContext and pObjectData in place\\n\\nSpecial Cases:\\n- Output buffer limited to 0x20 (32) bytes\\n- Performs automatic zero-padding if export yields less than 32 bytes\\n- Uses implicit EAX parameter from caller for additional data initialization",
