      "name": "ValidateHmacSignature",
      "signature": "bool ValidateHmacSignature(CryptoState * pCryptoContext, uint * pExpectedSignature)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "bool",
      "comment": "Validates HMAC-SHA1 signature by computing and comparing 5-dword signature against expected value.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize cryptographic object handles for key, IV, salt, and secret materials\n2. Import 32-byte key material from context offset +0x20\n3. Import 32-byte salt from context offset +0x80\n4. Import 32-byte IV from context offset +0xa0\n5. Import 32-byte secret from context offset +0x60\n6. Process cryptographic object collection with imported materials\n7. Cleanup temporary object handles\n8. Export computed hash to 32-byte buffer (exportedHashBuffer)\n9. Validate exported hash size (must be <= 0x20 bytes)\n10. Zero-pad remaining hash bytes if exported size < 32 bytes\n11. Compute dual-channel SHA1 hash of context at +0xc0\n12. Compute HMAC-SHA1 signature using key material from context offsets\n13. Compare computed signature (at context +0xe8) against expected signature\n14. Return true only if all 5 dwords match\n\nParameters:\n  pCryptoContext (CryptoState *): Cryptographic context buffer with embedded key materials:\n    +0x20: 32-byte key data\n    +0x40: 32-byte context data (dual-channel hash target)\n    +0x60: 32-byte secret material\n    +0x80: 32-byte salt data\n    +0xa0: 32-byte initialization vector\n    +0xc0: 32-byte hash output buffer (dual-channel result)\n    +0xe8: 20-byte computed HMAC-SHA1 signature (5 dwords)\n  pExpectedSignature (uint *): Pointer to expected 5-dword signature for comparison\n\nReturns:\n  true: All 5 signature dwords match expected values (valid signature)\n  false: At least one signature dword differs from expected (invalid signature)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Hash size check at offset +0x1c uses 0x20 as maximum for padding logic\n  - Zero-padding occurs only if exported hash size < 32 bytes\n  - CMPSD.REPE instruction performs 5-dword memcmp for efficient comparison\n  - __stdcall convention: caller removes 8 bytes (RET 0x8)\n  - All temporary object handles destroyed before signature computation\n  - Signature comparison loop uses CMPSD.REPE for early exit on mismatch",
