      "name": "SecureDeleteStrings",
      "signature": "void SecureDeleteStrings(char * param_1, char * param_2)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined",
      "comment": "Securely deletes two string buffers by zeroing memory before deallocation.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Test if first string pointer is non-null, skip to check_second_string if null\n2. Call strlen() to get length of first string\n3. Calculate number of 4-byte chunks (length >> 2) and store in ECX\n4. Clear register for zeroing (XOR EAX, EAX)\n5. Set EDI to first string address\n6. Use REP STOSD to zero memory in 4-byte chunks for efficiency\n7. Calculate remaining bytes (length & 3) and zero using REP STOSB\n8. Call DeleteObjectTrampoline() with source file reference (grid.cpp:440)\n9. Test if second string pointer is non-null, skip to return_cleanup if null\n10. Repeat steps 2-8 for second string (grid.cpp:446)\n11. Restore EDI register and return\n\nParameters:\nparam_1 (Stack[0x4]) - First string buffer pointer to securely delete (or NULL)\nparam_2 (Stack[0x8]) - Second string buffer pointer to securely delete (or NULL)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value, both strings are freed after secure zeroing\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- NULL pointers are safely handled with TEST/JZ checks\n- Memory is zeroed in 4-byte chunks (REP STOSD) for performance\n- Remaining bytes (0-3) are zeroed individually (REP STOSB)\n- Source file metadata passed to DeleteObjectTrampoline for debugging\n- Magic values 0x1b8 and 0x1be are source line numbers in grid.cpp",
