      "name": "InitializeBNCacheSystem",
      "signature": "uint InitializeBNCacheSystem(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Initializes the Battle.net cache file system for data storage and validation.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Enter critical section for thread-safe cache initialization\n2. Check if cache already initialized; clear if needed\n3. Build cache file path and create/open file with write access\n4. If file creation fails, clean up and return error\n5. Allocate memory for cache control block and I/O buffers\n6. Check existing file size; if invalid, create new cache from scratch\n   a. Initialize file with BN3 magic header (0x424E33, 0x1A)\n   b. Allocate and initialize first cache hash table (0x4000 bytes, 0x1000 entries)\n   c. Allocate and initialize second cache entry table (0x4000 bytes, 0x1000 entries)\n   d. Set all entries to 0xFFFFFFFF (invalid/uninitialized)\n7. For existing valid file:\n   a. Read 0x2c-byte header and validate BN2 or BN3 magic\n   b. Validate header CRC32 checksum\n   c. Validate all required header fields are non-zero\n   d. Read first cache table data with CRC32 validation\n   e. Apply hash table transformation to first table data\n   f. Read second cache table data with CRC32 validation\n   g. Apply hash table transformation to second table data\n8. Mark cache as initialized via global flag\n9. Leave critical section and return 1 on success, 0 on failure\n\nParameters:\nNone. This function operates on global cache state and synchronization objects.\n\nReturns:\n1 = Cache system initialized successfully\n0 = Cache initialization failed (invalid file, I/O error, memory allocation failure)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- BN2 format (0x1A324E42) is converted to BN3 format automatically\n- Cache files larger than 10MB (0xa00000) are treated as corrupted\n- Header CRC32 is calculated over bytes 0x00-0x1F with bytes 0x20-0x23 zeroed\n- Table entries 0x400-0xFFF are initialized to 0xFFFFFFFF to mark as unused\n- Hash table transformations are applied to loaded cache data for obfuscation",
