      "name": "InitializeNetworkFlag",
      "signature": "void InitializeNetworkFlag(uint * pNetworkSocket)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Initializes a network socket handle with the global singleton value.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate input parameter pNetworkSocket is not NULL via TEST ECX,ECX\n2. If NULL pointer detected, retrieve assertion source line 0x280 (640 decimal)\n3. Call AssertFailed with error context message at 0x6ff3a813 and line number\n4. Terminate process with exit(-1) after assertion failure\n5. If pointer valid, load global handle value from g_hNetworkSocket (0x6ff3f1f8)\n6. Dereference pointer and store handle value (1) into target location\n7. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\npNetworkSocket - Pointer to DWORD socket handle field to initialize (passed in ECX via __fastcall)\n                Type: uint * (32-bit pointer to unsigned 32-bit integer)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value. Function either initializes the socket handle or terminates via assertion.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- NULL pointer parameter triggers defensive assertion at line 640 (0x280 hex)\n- Error message string at 0x6ff3a813 contains assertion context information\n- Global socket handle g_hNetworkSocket contains constant value 1 (0x00000001)\n- Process termination via _exit(-1) is fatal and cannot be recovered\n- Called during Battle.net connection initialization sequences\n\nEdge Cases:\n- Function does not handle already-initialized handles (overwrites without checking)\n- No return value to indicate success/failure; relies on assertion for error reporting\n- Calling with invalid pointer will cause assertion failure and immediate termination",
