      "name": "ReceiveSocketData",
      "signature": "int ReceiveSocketData(SOCKET hSocket, char * pszBuffer, uint dwBufferSize)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Receives a specified number of bytes from a socket into a buffer using blocking I/O.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize total bytes received counter to 0\n2. Check if bufferSize is non-zero; if zero, skip loop and return success\n3. Loop until all requested bytes are received:\n   a. Calculate remaining bytes needed (bufferSize - totalBytesReceived)\n   b. Calculate current buffer offset (pBuffer + totalBytesReceived)\n   c. Call recv() with socket, buffer pointer, remaining size, flags=0\n   d. Check if recv() returned -1 (error condition)\n   e. Check if recv() returned 0 or negative (connection closed or error)\n   f. If error or closed, jump to error handler and return 0\n   g. Add bytes received to total counter\n   h. Compare total received against requested size\n   i. Loop if total < requested size, continue receiving\n4. Return 1 (success) when all bytes received or size was 0\n5. Return 0 (failure) on socket error or premature connection close\n\nParameters:\n  socket: SOCKET handle for network connection (stack parameter)\n  pBuffer: char* pointer to destination buffer (stack parameter)\n  bufferSize: uint number of bytes to receive (stack parameter)\n\nReturns:\n  1 - Successfully received all requested bytes\n  0 - Socket error (recv returned -1) or connection closed (recv returned 0)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - bufferSize of 0: Returns success immediately without calling recv()\n  - Uses blocking recv() call (flags=0), waits for data\n  - Continues looping until exact byte count received\n  - Any recv() error or zero-byte return triggers immediate failure\n  - Socket handle must be valid (no validation performed in function)\n\nCalling Convention:\n  __stdcall - Callee cleans stack (RET 0x4)\n  All parameters passed on stack following standard __stdcall convention",
