      "name": "HandlePatchProcessingResult",
      "signature": "int HandlePatchProcessingResult(int * pResultCode)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Interprets patch processing result codes and determines success/failure status.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Store pResultCode pointer reference in pResultCodeRef register\n2. Call ProcessPatchFile to execute patch operations with result destination\n3. Load result code value returned by ProcessPatchFile\n4. Test if result code equals 0 (patch failed or not needed)\n5. If result is 0, jump to return_failure (return 1)\n6. Test if result code equals 1 (patch already applied)\n7. If result is 1, jump to return_failure (return 1)\n8. Decrement result and test if equals 2 (patch applied, restart needed)\n9. If result is not 2, skip output assignment\n10. If result is 2, set *pResultCode to 1 (signal restart required)\n11. Clear EAX and return 0 (success)\n12. Or return 1 (failure) if result was 0 or 1\n\nParameters:\npResultCode - Pointer to integer that receives the patch processing result from ProcessPatchFile\n  Result value semantics:\n  0 = Patch processing failed or patch not needed\n  1 = Patch already previously applied\n  2 = Patch applied successfully, system restart required\n\nReturns:\nint - Success/Failure indicator for patch processing operation\n  0 = Patch processing successful (result code 2, or any other value)\n  1 = Patch processing failed (result code 0 or 1, indicating error or already patched)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Result code 2 is special: indicates successful patch with restart required\n- Output parameter (*pResultCode) is modified to 1 only when result code is exactly 2\n- All result codes other than 0, 1, and 2 are treated as success (return 0)\n- Function uses __fastcall: pResultCode passed in ECX register",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
      "method": "MNE",
      "index": "MNE:8045d7de5dd6411507a04d9f052d53ca",
