      "name": "CopyToGlobalBuffer",
      "signature": "void CopyToGlobalBuffer(void * pSrcBuffer, uint dwBufferSize)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Copies data from source buffer to globally allocated buffer.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load existing global buffer pointer from g_pGlobalBuffer\n2. If buffer is already allocated (not NULL), free existing buffer type 0x51c\n3. Allocate new buffer of type 0x51d (exactly 1309 bytes) via AllocateBuffer\n4. Store newly allocated buffer pointer to g_pGlobalBuffer global variable\n5. Copy source data using optimized strategy: divide buffer size by 4\n6. Copy full DWORDs (4 bytes) using REP MOVSD instruction for bulk data\n7. Calculate remainder bytes (size AND 3) for unaligned tail\n8. Copy remaining 1-3 bytes individually using REP MOVSB instruction\n9. Restore ESI and EDI registers from stack\n10. Return to caller with stack frame cleaned (RET 0x4)\n\nParameters:\npSourceBuffer (void*) - Pointer to source data buffer (passed in ECX per __fastcall)\nbufferSize (uint) - Number of bytes to copy from source (passed in EDX per __fastcall)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Function has no return value; side effect updates global buffer slot\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Buffer type 0x51d allocates exactly 1309 bytes regardless of requested size\n- Previous buffer freed if it exists, allowing safe buffer replacement\n- Optimized copy strategy minimizes CPU cycles for large buffers\n- Calling convention __fastcall passes parameters in ECX/EDX registers",
