      "name": "SHA1Update",
      "signature": "void SHA1Update(uint * pHashState)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Processes a 512-bit message block through the SHA-1 cryptographic hash algorithm, updating the hash state.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load 64 message bytes as 16 big-endian 32-bit words W[0..15] by reading 4 bytes at a time\n2. Expand message schedule: compute W[16..79] using XOR of W[i-3] XOR W[i-8] XOR W[i-14] XOR W[i-16], rotated left 1 bit\n3. Initialize working variables from hash state: A=H0, B=H1, C=H2, D=H3, E=H4\n4. Execute 80 rounds in four phases with different boolean functions and constants:\n   Rounds 0-19: f(B,C,D) = (~B AND D) | (B AND C), K = 0x5a827999\n   Rounds 20-39: f(B,C,D) = B XOR C XOR D, K = 0x6ed9eba1\n   Rounds 40-59: f(B,C,D) = (B AND C) | (B AND D) | (C AND D), K = 0x8f1bbcdc\n   Rounds 60-79: f(B,C,D) = B XOR C XOR D, K = 0xca62c1d6\n5. Each round computes: T = (A<<<5) + f(B,C,D) + E + W[t] + K, then rotates working variables\n6. Finalize: add computed values back to input hash state H[0..4] to complete block processing\n\nParameters:\npHashState: Pointer to 5 consecutive 32-bit unsigned integers containing the current hash state (H0, H1, H2, H3, H4)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - The function modifies the hash state in place by reference; no return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\nMessage block must be exactly 512 bits (64 bytes); SHA-1 is designed for fixed block sizes.\nThe message expansion formula uses four consecutive words and XOR operations to increase\ncryptographic diffusion. Round constants and boolean functions are selected to maximize\nthe avalanche effect where single-bit changes in input produce uncorrelated output changes.",
