      "name": "strtok",
      "signature": "char * strtok(char * szInputString, char * szSeparators)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "comment": "Splits a string into tokens based on separator characters (strtok implementation).\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load XOR security cookie from global g_dwSecurityCookie and XOR with stack frame pointer\n2. Allocate 32-byte separator bitmap on stack (256-bit map for all byte values)\n3. Call __getptd() to retrieve pointer to thread-local data structure for token state\n4. Scan separator string and mark each separator character in the bitmap using bit-shifting\n5. Check if pStr is NULL; if so, use saved token position from thread-local storage\n6. Skip leading separator characters by testing each byte against the bitmap\n7. Scan string forward until a separator character or null terminator is found\n8. If separator found, null-terminate the current token and advance position\n9. Save updated position in thread-local storage for next strtok() call\n10. Validate XOR security cookie to detect stack buffer overflows\n11. Return pointer to the current token, or NULL if no more tokens\n\nParameters:\n  pStr: Pointer to string to tokenize; NULL to continue from previous position\n  pSeparators: Pointer to null-terminated string of separator characters\n\nReturns:\n  char*: Pointer to next token string, or NULL when all tokens have been processed\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Separator bitmap uses bit-shifting for efficient O(1) lookup of 256 possible byte values\n  - Thread-local storage (_ptiddata._token) maintains state across multiple calls\n  - XOR canary check protects against stack-based buffer overflow attacks\n  - Function correctly handles edge cases: empty strings, all separators, consecutive separators\n  - First call with non-NULL pStr initializes tokenization; subsequent calls use NULL\n\nThread-Local Data Structure:\n  _ptiddata structure member at offset +0x18 (_token) stores current token position",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
